---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { analyticsId } from '../data/site-settings.mjs';
---
<Layout title="Privacy, cookies, and site notices" description="How the companion handles optional analytics, privacy choices, corrections, and rights in website and book material.">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Site notices · Updated 24 September 2026</p><h1>Privacy and use of this site.</h1><p class="lead">The companion can be read without an account. Optional analytics is separate from access to the chapters, references, and reading notes.</p></div>
  <div class="wrap prose long-copy">
    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy notice</h2>
    <p>This is a website of Natalie M. Scala and James P. Howard, II. The chapter and contributor searches run in your browser, and the site has no account system, advertising, or server-side search service. Search words can appear in the page address so that a search can be bookmarked or shared. A shared address therefore includes the search itself. The prepared production deployment uses GitHub Pages for hosting and Cloudflare for domain services. Hosting and domain providers may process request information such as an IP address, browser information, requested address, and time to deliver and protect the site. Their processing is described in the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub privacy statement</a> and <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.</p>
    {analyticsId ? <p>Google Analytics is configured as an optional service. Its tag is blocked until you accept analytics in the consent controls. Rejection does not send a cookieless analytics or consent ping to Google. After acceptance, Google can receive usage and device information and set analytics cookies. The configuration disables advertising personalization and Google signals, and does not pass the search query or fragment as page-location data. Google’s processing is described in its <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">privacy policy</a>. You may withdraw your choice in Cookie Settings; withdrawing removes accessible analytics cookies and reloads the page without the tag.</p> : <p><strong>Google Analytics is not enabled in this version.</strong> No measurement ID is configured, so accepting a historical preference cannot load a Google tag. There are no optional tracking cookies to accept. Enabling analytics later requires a separate configuration and a new affirmative choice for that configuration; it does not reuse a choice made while analytics was disabled.</p>}
    <p>Email sent through the contact link is handled by your email provider and the receiving mailbox. Information you include will be used to respond to the request and investigate the reported issue. Please avoid sending confidential records. Publisher and editor websites are separate services with their own privacy practices; opening a link takes you to that service. The companion loads its cover and identity images locally rather than asking the original image providers to serve each visit.</p>
    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and browser storage</h2>
    <p>The consent controls use a first-party cookie named <code>defenseor-consent</code> when you make an analytics choice. It records the decision, policy/configuration version, and expiration, not a visitor identifier. It can last up to 365 days, is scoped to this host, uses SameSite=Lax, and is marked Secure on HTTPS. Removing it in your browser removes the remembered choice. With no analytics configured and no previous choice, simply browsing the site does not create a preference cookie. No local-storage database is used. A browser’s Global Privacy Control signal keeps optional analytics off.</p>
    <p><a href="#cookies" data-cookie-preferences>Open Cookie Settings</a>. With JavaScript disabled, no analytics tag loads; browser settings can still be used to remove any earlier cookies.</p>
    <h2 id="terms">Using the companion</h2>
    <p>The website supports reading, teaching, and finding the handbook’s contributions. The material is educational and should be assessed in light of the assumptions, dates, and scope of the relevant source. It is not operational direction or a substitute for professional judgment. Links to the publisher identify where the published book or chapter can be obtained; this site does not guarantee access through a subscription or account held elsewhere. Please report errors or access problems through <a href="/contact/">Contact and corrections</a>.</p>
    <h2 id="copyright">Copyright and attribution</h2>
    <p>Copyright © 2018–{new Date().getFullYear()} Natalie M. Scala and James P. Howard, II, for the original website material. Published chapters, book covers, photographs, and other supplied material retain their respective rights. Chapter overviews and reading-room exercises are identified as companion writing, separate from the chapter authors’ work. Providing a citation, a print button, or a download does not grant rights in the publisher’s chapter text or illustrations. For reuse beyond applicable permissions, contact the relevant rights holder. <a href="/about/#editorial">About the editorial material</a> explains how the layers of authorship are distinguished.</p>
  </div>
</Layout>
